```js
// var: function-scoped, hoisted and initialized with undefined, re-declarable
console.log(a); // undefined (hoisted)
var a = 1;

// let: block-scoped, hoisted but NOT initialized -> temporal dead zone (TDZ)
// console.log(b); // ReferenceError: Cannot access 'b' before initialization
let b = 2;
b = 3; // reassignment OK

// const: block-scoped, must be initialized at declaration, cannot be reassigned
const c = 4;
// c = 5; // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable
// NOTE: const objects are still mutable in their contents
const user = { name: 'Sara' };
user.name = 'Ali'; // OK

// Classic var-in-loop pitfall vs let
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => console.log('var:', i));   // 3, 3, 3
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) setTimeout(() => console.log('let:', j));   // 0, 1, 2
```
Key rules:
- `var` is function-scoped and hoisted with the value `undefined`; redeclaration is allowed.
- `let`/`const` are block-scoped; they are hoisted but stay in the temporal dead zone until their declaration line, so touching them earlier throws a ReferenceError.
- Default to `const`; use `let` only when you must reassign; avoid `var` in modern code.

```js
function createCounter(start = 0) {
  let count = start; // private variable captured by the closure
  return {
    increment() { return ++count; },
    decrement() { return --count; },
    value() { return count; }
  };
}

const counter = createCounter(10);
console.log(counter.increment()); // 11
console.log(counter.increment()); // 12
console.log(counter.value());     // 12
counter.count = 99;               // does NOT touch the real count
console.log(counter.value());     // still 12

// Closures also power factory functions:
const makeGreeter = (greeting) => (name) => `${greeting}, ${name}!`;
const sayHello = makeGreeter('Hello');
console.log(sayHello('Arion')); // Hello, Arion!
```
A closure is a function bundled with references to its surrounding lexical scope. Even after the outer function returns, inner functions keep access to its variables. Use closures for private state, factories and callbacks. Two caveats: closures capture variables (not values), so a variable that changes later is seen by every closure over it; and closures keep their captured variables in memory as long as the inner function is referenced.

```js
// ES6 class (syntactic sugar over prototypes)
class Animal {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  speak() { return `${this.name} makes a sound`; }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  speak() { return `${this.name} barks`; }
}

const rex = new Dog('Rex');
console.log(rex.speak());          // Rex barks
console.log(rex instanceof Dog);   // true
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(rex) === Dog.prototype); // true

// The equivalent pre-ES6 prototype style
function AnimalOld(name) { this.name = name; }
AnimalOld.prototype.speak = function () { return `${this.name} makes a sound`; };

function DogOld(name) { AnimalOld.call(this, name); }   // inherit instance fields
DogOld.prototype = Object.create(AnimalOld.prototype);  // inherit methods
DogOld.prototype.constructor = DogOld;
DogOld.prototype.speak = function () { return `${this.name} barks`; };

const rexOld = new DogOld('Rex');
console.log(rexOld.speak()); // Rex barks
```
- Classes are NOT a new object model: `class Dog {}` creates a function whose `prototype` object holds the methods, and instances link to it via their internal `[[Prototype]]`.
- `extends` sets up the prototype chain plus `super()` handling; `Object.create` is the manual equivalent in the old style.
- Differences: class declarations are not usable before their definition (no hoisting for them), class bodies always run in strict mode, and class methods are non-enumerable.

```js
// 1. Plain call: this is undefined (strict mode) or window (sloppy mode)
function whereAmI() { return this; }

// 2. Method call: this = the object before the dot
const user = {
  name: 'Sara',
  greet() { return `Hi, ${this.name}`; },
  delayed() {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(this.greet()), 100); // arrow inherits this = user
  }
};
console.log(user.greet()); // Hi, Sara

const loose = user.greet;   // method extracted from its object
console.log(loose());       // Hi, undefined — receiver lost

// 3. Arrow functions have NO own this — they use the enclosing scope's
user.delayed(); // Hi, Sara (an ordinary function here would log undefined)

// 4. Explicit binding with call / apply / bind
function greet2(greeting, punct) { return `${greeting}, ${this.name}${punct}`; }

greet2.call(user, 'Hi', '!');     // Hi, Sara!   (args listed one by one)
greet2.apply(user, ['Hey', '?']); // Hey, Sara?  (args as an array)
const bound = greet2.bind(user);  // permanent binding
console.log(bound('Yo', '.'));    // Yo, Sara.
```
Binding priority: (1) `new` binds `this` to the fresh instance; (2) explicit `call`/`apply`/`bind`; (3) implicit `obj.method()`; (4) default — `undefined` in strict mode. Arrow functions ignore all of these and take `this` from their defining scope. The classic bug: passing a method as a callback (`el.addEventListener('click', obj.handle)`) drops the receiver — fix with `.bind(obj)`, an arrow wrapper, or a class field arrow function.

```js
// Object destructuring
const user = { id: 7, name: 'Sara', address: { city: 'Tehran', zip: '12345' } };
const { name, id, role = 'guest', city } = { ...user, ...user.address }; // role default + nested city
console.log(name, id, role, city); // Sara 7 guest Tehran

// Renaming
const { name: fullName } = user;
console.log(fullName); // Sara

// Array destructuring
const rgb = [255, 128, 0];
const [r, g, b] = rgb;
const [first, , third] = rgb;             // skip items
const [head = 0, ...tail] = rgb;          // rest elements
console.log(r, g, b, first, third, tail); // 255 128 0 255 0 [128, 0]

// Swapping without a temp variable
let x = 1, y = 2;
[x, y] = [y, x];

// Function parameter destructuring (common for options objects)
function draw({ x = 0, y = 0, color = 'black' } = {}) {
  return `circle at ${x},${y} in ${color}`;
}
console.log(draw({ x: 10 })); // circle at 10,0 in black
```
Destructuring pulls values out of arrays/objects into variables in one step. Patterns work at any depth, support defaults, renaming (`{ a: alias }`), rest elements, and defaulting the whole parameter with `= {}` so the function stays callable with no arguments.

```js
// Spread: expands iterables/objects
const a = [1, 2, 3];
const b = [...a, 4, 5];              // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] (copy + extend)
const copy = [...a];                 // shallow copy
console.log(Math.max(...a));         // 3 (spread into function args)

const base = { theme: 'dark', lang: 'en' };
const overrides = { lang: 'fa' };
const config = { ...base, ...overrides }; // later keys win -> { theme: 'dark', lang: 'fa' }

// Rest: collects the remainder
const [first, ...others] = b;        // first = 1, others = [2, 3, 4, 5]
const { theme, ...rest } = config;   // theme = 'dark', rest = { lang: 'fa' }

function sum(...numbers) {           // rest parameter: a real array
  return numbers.reduce((t, n) => t + n, 0);
}
console.log(sum(1, 2, 3, 4));        // 10
```
- Spread (`...`) writes: it expands an array/object into another literal or arguments into a call. It makes shallow copies only — nested objects are still shared.
- Rest (`...`) reads: it gathers remaining elements/properties into a fresh array/object, or collects function arguments.
Same three dots, opposite directions: spread unpacks, rest packs.

```js
// Map: keys of ANY type, insertion order, direct size
const visits = new Map();
visits.set('sara', 3);
visits.set(42, 'answer');
visits.set({ id: 1 }, 'object keys work');
console.log(visits.get('sara'), visits.size); // 3 3
console.log(visits.has(42));                  // true
visits.delete(42);
for (const [key, value] of visits) console.log(key, value);

// Convert between objects and Maps
const obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };
const m = new Map(Object.entries(obj));       // obj -> Map
const back = Object.fromEntries(m);           // Map -> obj

// Set: unique values, O(1) add/has/delete
const ids = new Set([1, 2, 2, 3]);
console.log(ids.size);                        // 3
ids.add(3).add(4);
console.log([...ids]);                        // [1, 2, 3, 4]

const uniq = arr => [...new Set(arr)];
console.log(uniq(['a', 'b', 'a']));           // ['a', 'b']

const setA = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const common = [1, 2, 3].filter(x => new Set([2, 3, 4]).has(x)); // intersection [2, 3]
```
Use `Map` when keys are not strings, when you add/delete keys frequently, need `size`, or iterate in insertion order. Use `Set` for deduplication and membership tests — much cleaner than `arr.indexOf(x) !== -1`. Object keys are always strings/symbols; Map keys keep their type (even objects and NaN).

```js
// math.js — named exports
export const PI = 3.14159;
export function area(r) { return PI * r * r; }
export default function clamp(v, min, max) { // one default per module
  return Math.min(Math.max(v, min), max);
}

// utils.js — re-export in one line
export { PI, area } from './math.js';
export { default as clamp } from './math.js';

// app.js — importing
import clamp from './math.js';                    // default: any local name
import { PI, area } from './math.js';             // named: exact names
import { area as circleArea } from './math.js';   // rename on import
import * as math from './math.js';                // namespace object

console.log(area(2), PI, clamp(99, 0, 10), math.area(1));
```
```html
<script type='module' src='app.js'></script>
```
Notes:
- Named exports/imports use curly braces and must match names; there can be only one `default` export per module.
- Modules are strict mode, deferred by default, execute once (singletons), and have their own top-level scope — no more global `var` leaks.
- Imports are hoisted and are live bindings: if the exporting module changes an exported `let`, importers see the new value.
- In the browser use `<script type='module'>`; dynamic `import('./x.js')` returns a promise, which enables code splitting.

```js
// A promise wraps async work: pending -> fulfilled | rejected (then settled)
function wait(ms, value) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => (value !== undefined ? resolve(value) : reject(new Error('no value'))), ms);
  });
}

wait(300, 'done')
  .then(v => { console.log(v); return wait(200, 'chained'); }) // return a promise to chain
  .then(v => console.log(v))
  .catch(err => console.error('caught:', err.message))         // catches anything above
  .finally(() => console.log('always runs'));

// Combinators
const p1 = wait(100, 1), p2 = wait(200, 2);
Promise.all([p1, p2]).then(console.log);         // [1, 2] — rejects fast if ANY fails
Promise.allSettled([p1, p2]).then(console.log);  // [{status:'fulfilled',...}, ...] never rejects
Promise.race([p1, p2]).then(console.log);        // 1 — first to settle wins
Promise.any([p1, p2]).then(console.log);         // 1 — first FULFILLED wins
```
- A promise is pending, then settles exactly once (fulfilled or rejected) — it can never settle twice.
- `.then()` returns a NEW promise, so chains flatten; throwing inside a `.then` skips to the nearest `.catch` below it.
- `Promise.all` fails fast on the first rejection; `allSettled` waits for every outcome; `race` settles with the first settled; `any` settles with the first success and rejects only if all fail.

```js
async function getUser(id) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`); // await pauses THIS function, not the page
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

// Sequential (slower: the second request waits for the first)
async function sequential() {
  const a = await getUser(1);
  const b = await getUser(2);
  return [a, b];
}

// Parallel (faster: both requests are in flight together)
async function parallel() {
  const [a, b] = await Promise.all([getUser(1), getUser(2)]);
  return [a, b];
}

// Error handling: sync throws and rejected awaits are caught the same way
async function run() {
  try {
    const user = await getUser(999);
    console.log(user);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed:', err.message); // HTTP 404, network error, bad JSON...
  } finally {
    console.log('cleanup here');
  }
}
run();
```
Key points: `async` functions always return a promise; `await` works on any thenable; `try/catch` handles both sync and async errors inside the function. Never `await` in a loop for independent requests — use `Promise.all`. And always consume the promise an async function returns; a floating call with no `await`/`.catch` can produce an unhandled rejection.

```js
async function fetchJSON(url, options = {}) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 8000); // 8s timeout
  try {
    const res = await fetch(url, { ...options, signal: controller.signal });

    // fetch does NOT throw on HTTP errors (404/500) — check res.ok yourself
    if (!res.ok) {
      const body = await res.text().catch(() => '');
      throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status} ${res.statusText}: ${body.slice(0, 100)}`);
    }
    return await res.json(); // throws SyntaxError if the body is not valid JSON
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') throw new Error('Request timed out');
    throw err; // network failure (TypeError: Failed to fetch) or parse error
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timeoutId);
  }
}

// Usage
fetchJSON('/api/items')
  .then(items => render(items))
  .catch(err => showError(err.message));
```
Critical facts: fetch only rejects on network failures — a 404 or 500 resolves normally, so you MUST check `response.ok` (true for status 200-299). Parse with `res.json()` or `res.text()`; both consume the body, which can be read only once. Add `AbortController` for timeouts and cancellation, and distinguish `err.name === 'AbortError'` from real failures.

```js
console.log('1 sync');

setTimeout(() => console.log('2 timeout'), 0);      // macrotask (timer queue)

Promise.resolve()
  .then(() => console.log('3 microtask'))           // microtask queue
  .then(() => console.log('4 microtask'));

queueMicrotask(() => console.log('5 microtask'));

console.log('6 sync');
// Output: 1, 6, 3, 4, 5, 2
```
How it works:
1. The call stack runs all synchronous code first (`1`, `6`).
2. When the stack empties, the event loop drains the ENTIRE microtask queue (promise callbacks, `queueMicrotask`, `MutationObserver`) before touching anything else (`3`, `4`, `5`).
3. Then it takes one macrotask (`setTimeout`/`setInterval`, I/O, UI events — here `2`), runs it, and again drains all microtasks before the next macrotask.
Consequences: `setTimeout(fn, 0)` never runs before pending microtasks; a long-running sync loop blocks rendering and event handling; `await` is microtask-based, so `await Promise.resolve()` also just yields to the microtask queue. Rendering happens between macrotasks after microtasks — that is why stacking many `.then` calls still delays the next paint.

