```js
// One observer, many targets — far cheaper than scroll listeners
const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries, obs) => {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
    entry.target.classList.add('visible');
    obs.unobserve(entry.target); // animate once, then stop watching
  }
}, { threshold: 0.15, rootMargin: '0px 0px -50px 0px' });

document.querySelectorAll('.reveal').forEach(el => revealObserver.observe(el));

// Lazy loading: swap data-src for src when the image approaches the viewport
const imgObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries, obs) => {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (entry.isIntersecting) {
      entry.target.src = entry.target.dataset.src;
      entry.target.removeAttribute('data-src');
      obs.unobserve(entry.target);
    }
  }
}, { rootMargin: '200px' }); // start loading 200px before it becomes visible

document.querySelectorAll('img.lazy').forEach(img => imgObserver.observe(img));
```
`IntersectionObserver` asynchronously reports when elements cross the viewport (the `root`) — no scroll-event math, no layout thrashing. Key options: `threshold` (0 = any pixel, 1 = fully visible), `rootMargin` (grow/shrink the root box), `root` (defaults to the viewport). Each callback receives entries with `isIntersecting`, `intersectionRatio` and `target`. Call `unobserve` after one-shot work, and remember the callback fires once immediately with the current state when you call `observe()`.

```js
const canvas = document.querySelector('canvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

const ro = new ResizeObserver(entries => {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    // contentBoxSize ignores borders/padding; borderBoxSize includes them
    const { inlineSize: width, blockSize: height } = entry.contentBoxSize[0];
    canvas.width = width;   // canvas pixels = CSS size -> crisp rendering
    canvas.height = height;
    drawChart(width, height);
  }
});

ro.observe(canvas.parentElement); // react to the CONTAINER, not the window

function drawChart(w, h) {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, w, h);
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.moveTo(0, h / 2);
  ctx.lineTo(w, h / 2);
  ctx.stroke();
}

// Cleanup when the component goes away
// ro.disconnect();
```
`ResizeObserver` fires whenever an observed element's content box changes size — including container-driven changes that never touch the window (sidebar collapse, flex reflow, font loading). That is the foundation of container-responsive components, which media queries cannot express. Notes: the callback runs after layout but before paint (no flicker); it fires once on `observe()` with the initial size; entries arrive as an array (batched changes, one callback). Guard against resize loops: never change the observed element's own size inside its callback without a size check.

```js
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(field, message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';  // appears in stack traces
    this.field = field;
    this.status = 400;
  }
}

class ApiError extends Error {
  constructor(status, body) {
    super(`API responded with ${status}`);
    this.name = 'ApiError';
    this.status = status;
    this.body = body;
  }
}

function validateEmail(email) {
  if (!/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)) {
    throw new ValidationError('email', 'Invalid email format');
  }
}

async function saveUser(data) {
  try {
    validateEmail(data.email);  // sync throw
    const res = await fetch('/api/users', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(data) });
    if (!res.ok) throw new ApiError(res.status, await res.text());
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
      showFieldError(err.field, err.message); // recoverable: flag the input
      return null;
    }
    throw err; // unknown: rethrow, log at the top level
  } finally {
    console.log('runs on success, catch, AND rethrow');
  }
}
```
Extend `Error` and call `super(message)` so you get proper stacks and `instanceof` still works. Use distinct error types to separate handling decisions (retry a 503, flag a 400 field, crash on genuine bugs). `finally` runs no matter how the block exits — ideal for closing loaders or connections. Never swallow errors silently; catch only what you can meaningfully handle and rethrow the rest.

