**Symptom:** vertical margins vanish between elements, or a child's top margin moves the PARENT instead of the child.

**Root cause — margin collapsing:** adjacent vertical margins (and parent/first-child margins with nothing between them) merge into one; the largest wins:
```css
.card { background: #fff; }                /* no border/padding/overflow */
.card p:first-child { margin-top: 40px; }  /* collapses THROUGH the card */
```
The 40px escapes the card and pushes the whole card away from the element above it.

**Fix — create a barrier (block formatting context):**
```css
.card {
  background: #fff;
  display: flow-root;   /* modern, side-effect-free way to stop collapsing */
  /* overflow: hidden also works, but can clip shadows/dropdowns */
  /* padding-top: 1px or border-top also blocks that edge */
}
```

**Between siblings:** `h2 { margin-bottom: 20px } p { margin-top: 30px }` yields 30px, not 50px — they collapse to the larger value. Prefer `gap` over margins for spacing:
```css
.stack { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 24px; }
.stack > * { margin: 0; }
```

**Prevention:** use `gap` (flex/grid) instead of vertical margins between siblings; add `display: flow-root` to containers whose children need top margins; remember only vertical margins collapse, only in block layout (never in flex/grid), and horizontal margins never collapse.

**Symptom:** the click handler does nothing and there is no console error.

**Diagnosis — check these in order:**
```js
// 1. Element is null: the script ran before the DOM was parsed
const btn = document.querySelector('#save');
if (!btn) console.warn('#save not found — wrong selector or script too early');

// 2. Verify the handler actually attached
if (btn) {
  btn.onclick = () => console.log('clicked!');
  console.log(typeof btn.onclick); // 'function' proves the assignment worked
}

// 3. Something sits ON TOP of the button and eats the click
// DevTools: Ctrl+Shift+C over the button — if the highlighted node is an
// overlay/backdrop/::after, the button never receives the event
// Tracer:
document.addEventListener('click', e => console.log('hit:', e.target), true);

// 4. An ancestor handler calls stopPropagation before it reaches your listener
parent.addEventListener('click', e => e.stopPropagation()); // kills bubbling

// 5. The button is disabled — disabled buttons fire no click events
console.log(btn?.disabled);
```

**Fix:** load scripts with `defer` or place them before `</body>` so the DOM exists; re-check selector syntax (`#id` vs `.class`); remove or reposition overlapping overlays (inspect `z-index` and wrappers with `pointer-events`); audit stray `stopPropagation` calls.

**Prevention:** prefer `addEventListener` — repeated `onclick =` assignments silently overwrite each other; throw or warn when a queried element is missing during setup instead of failing silently; test in a clean browser profile where extensions (which inject overlays) are disabled.

**Symptom:** the page scrolls left-right on phones (often only below ~500px) or shows a stray white strip on the right.

**Diagnosis — find the offender first:**
```js
// Paste in the console: outlines every element wider than the viewport
document.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(el => {
  const w = document.documentElement.clientWidth;
  if (el.scrollWidth > w || el.getBoundingClientRect().right > w + 1) {
    console.log(el, el.scrollWidth, el.getBoundingClientRect().right);
    el.style.outline = '2px solid red';
  }
});
```
**Common root causes:**
- Fixed pixel widths: `width: 400px` on a card inside a 360px viewport.
- `width: 100vw` — vw INCLUDES the scrollbar width, so it is wider than the visible area.
- Long unbreakable strings (URLs, emails) with no wrapping.
- Negative margins or transforms poking content outside the body.
- Images or `pre` blocks with intrinsic width.

**Fix:**
```css
html, body { overflow-x: clip; }          /* safety net, not the cure */
img, video, pre { max-width: 100%; }
p, li, a { overflow-wrap: break-word; }
.card { width: 100%; max-width: 400px; }  /* fluid, capped — never bare px width */
/* Also let flex/grid children shrink: min-width: 0 on the offending item */
```
Replace `100vw` with `100%` (or use `100dvw` deliberately).

**Prevention:** design with `max-width` instead of `width`; test at 320px early; keep the offender-detection snippet handy and run it on every layout change.

**Symptom:** `transition: opacity 0.3s` is set, but flipping `display: none` to `display: block` shows no animation at all.

**Root cause:** `display` is not animatable, and a `display: none` element has no rendered box to transition FROM. The moment display flips, the element jumps from nonexistent to fully rendered in one frame — transitions need a computed-style change BETWEEN frames.

**Fixes:**
```css
/* Option 1: animate opacity + visibility (visibility IS transitionable) */
.panel {
  opacity: 0;
  visibility: hidden;
  transition: opacity 0.3s, visibility 0.3s;
  pointer-events: none;
}
.panel.open { opacity: 1; visibility: visible; pointer-events: auto; }
```
```js
// Option 2: if you truly need display, force a style flush between changes
const panel = document.querySelector('.panel');
panel.style.display = 'block';
void panel.offsetHeight;          // force reflow, THEN change the animated property
panel.classList.add('open');
```
```css
/* Option 3 (modern browsers): allow-discrete + @starting-style */
.panel { transition: opacity 0.3s, display 0.3s allow-discrete; }
@starting-style { .panel { opacity: 0; } }
```
Option 1 keeps occupying space — combine it with `grid-template-rows: 0fr -> 1fr` or a max-height animation when the element must also collapse.

**Prevention:** animate only compositable properties (`opacity`, `transform`); never pair `display` with `transition`; use the opacity+visibility pattern for show/hide, and `allow-discrete` with `@starting-style` in evergreen browsers.

**Symptom:**
```js
function getUser(id) {
  fetch(`/api/users/${id}`)
    .then(res => res.json())
    .then(data => console.log(data)); // logs the user fine
  // no return: the function ends here -> undefined
}
const user = getUser(1);
console.log(user); // undefined
```

**Root cause:** fetch is asynchronous — the function RETURNS before the response arrives, and without returning the promise (or awaiting it), the caller receives `undefined`. You cannot return async data synchronously; you must return the promise or consume it at the callsite.

**Fix:**
```js
// Option A: return the chain
function getUser(id) {
  return fetch(`/api/users/${id}`)
    .then(res => {
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
      return res.json(); // return INSIDE then, or the next link gets undefined
    });
}
getUser(1).then(user => console.log(user));

// Option B: async/await (clearer)
async function getUser(id) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  return res.json(); // async functions wrap the return value in a promise
}

const user = await getUser(1); // top-level await in modules, or inside another async fn
```
The sibling bug: `then(res => { res.json(); })` — an arrow with a body and no `return` hands `undefined` to the next `.then`.

**Prevention:** make every async function `async` and always `return`/`await` its sub-promises; never mix `.then` chains with missing returns; enable ESLint `consistent-return` and be suspicious of any function whose body logs but does not return.

**Symptom:** one click runs the handler twice — counter jumps by 2, duplicate toasts, double POST requests.

**Diagnosis — the usual four culprits:**
```js
// 1. Handler registered again on every render (hot reload, re-init, router hook)
function mount() {
  btn.addEventListener('click', buy); // runs on EACH mount -> handlers stack up
}
// Fix: reset listeners with an AbortController on every mount
let ctl;
function mount() {
  ctl?.abort();
  ctl = new AbortController();
  btn.addEventListener('click', buy, { signal: ctl.signal });
}

// 2. Bubbling: nested elements both listen to the same event type
outer.addEventListener('click', track); // click on inner bubbles up to outer
inner.addEventListener('click', track); // track runs twice per click
// Fix: attach once at the common ancestor and branch on e.target (delegation)

// 3. Button inside a form without type='button': click + implicit submit both fire
form.querySelector('button').addEventListener('click', save); // save runs, THEN form submits
// Fix: <button type='button'> for non-submit actions

// 4. Inline onclick AND addEventListener registered for the same event
```

**Fix:** initialize listeners exactly once per element lifetime; add `type='button'`; delegate at one ancestor instead of nested pairs; when re-init is unavoidable, guard with `if (btn.dataset.bound) return; btn.dataset.bound = '1';`.

**Prevention:** log `e.target` and `console.trace()` at the top of suspect handlers to see who double-fires; disable the button while a request is in flight; make critical server actions idempotent so double-fires cannot double-charge.

**Symptom:** on mobile, `height: 100vh` elements change size as the URL bar hides/shows while scrolling; full-screen sections are taller than what is actually visible.

**Root cause:** on mobile browsers `100vh` refers to the LARGEST viewport (toolbar hidden). While the toolbar is visible, the element is taller than the visible area; as you scroll, the browser hides/shows the toolbar, resizing the layout and causing the jump.

