Guidelines: spread/`Object.assign` copy one level — fine for flat state updates (React/Redux style). Use `structuredClone` (native since 2022) for deep copies; it throws on functions and DOM nodes. Avoid `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())` in production: it silently destroys Dates, `undefined`, functions and Maps, and throws on circular references. If you are cloning on every change at scale, consider immutable update patterns instead.

```js
// Default sort compares STRINGS — '10' < '2' because '1' < '2'
console.log([10, 2, 33, 1].sort());                 // [1, 10, 2, 33] — wrong for numbers

// Fix: comparator returning negative / zero / positive
console.log([10, 2, 33, 1].sort((a, b) => a - b));  // ascending: [1, 2, 10, 33]
console.log([10, 2, 33, 1].sort((a, b) => b - a));  // descending: [33, 10, 2, 1]

// Objects: compare the field
const users = [{ name: 'Ali', age: 30 }, { name: 'Sara', age: 25 }];
users.sort((a, b) => a.age - b.age);

// Strings: locale-aware, accent-correct
console.log(['zebra', 'Apple', 'banana'].sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b)));
// ['Apple', 'banana', 'zebra']

// Multi-field: by age, then by name
users.sort((a, b) => a.age - b.age || a.name.localeCompare(b.name));

// CRITICAL: sort() mutates the original array — copy first
const copy = [...users].sort((a, b) => b.age - a.age);
console.log(users === copy); // false; original untouched
console.log([3, 1, 2].toSorted()); // ES2023: sorts and returns a NEW array
```
The default comparator stringifies elements — fine for plain strings, broken for numbers (lexicographic order). Always pass `(a, b) => a - b` for numeric data. Remember `sort` mutates in place; use `[...arr].sort()` or `arr.toSorted()` when the original order matters.

