**Symptom:** `'a b c'.replace(' ', '-')` returns `'a-b c'` — only the first space changed.

**Root cause:** when `String.prototype.replace` receives a STRING pattern, it replaces only the first match — that is the documented behavior of the string overload, not a bug. All-match behavior requires a global regex or `replaceAll`.

**Fix:**
```js
const text = 'a b c';

console.log(text.replace(/ /g, '-'));    // 'a-b-c'  — global regex (classic)
console.log(text.replaceAll(' ', '-'));  // 'a-b-c'  — ES2021, clearest

// replaceAll with a regex REQUIRES the g flag
console.log(text.replaceAll(/ /g, '-')); // OK
// text.replaceAll(/ /, '-');            // TypeError: must be a global RegExp

// Dynamic strings are safest with replaceAll — no escaping needed:
const term = 'C++ (2024)';
console.log(text.replaceAll(term, 'X'));

// Replacement patterns: '$&' = matched text, '$1' = capture group,
// '$$' = a literal dollar sign
console.log('price'.replaceAll('price', '$$100')); // '$100'
```

**Prevention:** default to `replaceAll` for literal substrings and `/pattern/g` for patterns; never assume string `replace` is global; when building regexes from user input, escape special characters (`.` `*` `?` `+`) or stick to the string form.

**Symptom:** `SyntaxError: Unexpected token '<', "<html>" is not valid JSON` (or `Unexpected token o`, `Unexpected end of JSON input`).

**Root cause:** you are parsing something that is not JSON. `<` at position 0 means the server returned an HTML page (login redirect, 404/500 error page, SPA index.html fallback) — usually a wrong API path, an expired session, or a proxy answering instead of the backend. `Unexpected token o` usually means the value was already an object (stringified as `[object Object]`). `Unexpected end of input` means a truncated or empty body.

**Diagnosis:**
```js
const res = await fetch('/api/data');
console.log(res.status, res.headers.get('content-type'));
const raw = await res.text();      // look at the ACTUAL body first
console.log(raw.slice(0, 200));
const data = JSON.parse(raw);      // now parse
```

**Fix — parse defensively:**
```js
async function getJSON(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}: ${await res.text().catch(() => '')}`);
  const type = res.headers.get('content-type') ?? '';
  if (!type.includes('application/json')) {
    throw new Error(`Expected JSON, got ${type || 'unknown'}`);
  }
  return res.json();
}
```

**Prevention:** never call `res.json()` before checking `res.ok`; verify the route, method and auth headers; check `Content-Type` before parsing; handle `res.status === 204` explicitly since it has no body.

**Symptom:** the polling callback keeps firing after the panel is closed; `clearInterval(id)` seems to have no effect; the Network tab shows endless repeated requests.

**Root cause:** `clearInterval` only stops the timer whose exact id you pass. The id gets lost when a variable holding it is overwritten, when the interval is created in a scope that loses the id, or when a second interval is started without clearing the first — the old timer is orphaned and unreachable.

```js
// BROKEN: every open() starts ANOTHER interval; pollId only remembers the last
let pollId;
function open()  { pollId = setInterval(load, 2000); }
function close() { clearInterval(pollId); }
open(); open(); // two live timers, one lost id -> leak

// FIXED: clear before creating; track ids in a Set for bulk teardown
const timers = new Set();
function startPolling() {
  stopPolling();                       // never stack intervals
  timers.add(setInterval(load, 2000));
}
function stopPolling() {
  for (const id of timers) clearInterval(id);
  timers.clear();
}

// ALTERNATIVE: self-scheduling timeout — no overlaps, trivially cancellable
let alive = true, handle;
function loop() {
  if (!alive) return;
  load();
  handle = setTimeout(loop, 2000);
}
function stop() { alive = false; clearTimeout(handle); }
```
Two more gotchas: removing the DOM element does NOT clear timers (they are owned by the window, not the element), and hidden tabs only THROTTLE timers — they keep existing.

**Prevention:** give every `setInterval`/`setTimeout` an owner responsible for teardown (SPA route cleanup, unmount hooks); prefer self-scheduling `setTimeout` chains for polling; verify cleanup with the Performance panel or a counter inside the callback.

**Symptom:**
```js
const buttons = document.querySelectorAll('button');
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  buttons[i].onclick = () => console.log('button', i); // every click logs 'button 3'
}
```

**Root cause:** `var` is function-scoped, so all three arrow functions close over the SAME single `i`. By the time any click happens, the loop finished and `i` is 3 — closures capture variables, not values.

**Fixes:**
```js
// 1. let creates a NEW binding per iteration — one-line fix
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  buttons[i].onclick = () => console.log('button', i); // 0, 1, 2 — correct
}

// 2. IIFE: copy the value into a fresh scope (pre-ES6 pattern)
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  ((index) => {
    buttons[index].onclick = () => console.log('button', index);
  })(i);
}

// 3. Do not capture the index at all — read it from the element
buttons.forEach((btn, index) => {
  btn.onclick = () => console.log('button', index, btn.dataset.id);
});
```
The same trap hits `setTimeout(fn, 0)` inside `var` loops and any callback created in a loop body.

**Prevention:** use `let`/`const` everywhere (ESLint `no-var`); prefer `forEach`/`for...of` over indexed loops when binding handlers; remember the rule — closures capture the VARIABLE, so always ask whether that variable still changes after being captured.
